WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.092
SSRF prevention begins by minimizing the
network capability exposed to a requester.

2
00:00:05.172 --> 00:00:08.234
Prefer configured integrations, fixed
provider bases,

3
00:00:08.314 --> 00:00:11.950
and server-controlled destination mappings
over arbitrary URLs.

4
00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:15.283
Authorize the caller's use of that
integration separately.

5
00:00:15.363 --> 00:00:17.531
When user-provided URLs are essential,

6
00:00:17.611 --> 00:00:21.757
define a narrow operation and an explicit
destination policy before making a

7
00:00:21.837 --> 00:00:22.539
connection.

8
00:00:22.619 --> 00:00:24.660
Parse with a standards-aware URL API.

9
00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:28.631
Allow only required protocols, reject
unwanted embedded credentials,

10
00:00:28.711 --> 00:00:31.645
and authorize hostname and port as
structured values.

11
00:00:31.725 --> 00:00:36.126
These checks establish syntax and
application policy, not the network endpoint.

12
00:00:36.206 --> 00:00:40.735
Resolve hostnames through the intended
connection environment and classify every

13
00:00:40.815 --> 00:00:43.940
applicable IPv4 and IPv6 address with a
vetted mechanism.

14
00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:47.847
Include special-purpose and
deployment-specific forbidden ranges,

15
00:00:47.927 --> 00:00:50.606
and apply the same classification to IP
literals.

16
00:00:50.686 --> 00:00:53.301
A successful parse, trusted-looking
hostname,

17
00:00:53.381 --> 00:00:56.762
or authenticated caller never substitutes
for this decision.

18
00:00:56.842 --> 00:00:58.819
Treat every redirect as a new target.

19
00:00:58.899 --> 00:01:03.555
Repeat protocol, hostname, port,
resolution, and address policy before the next hop.

20
00:01:03.635 --> 00:01:07.781
Close the DNS check-and-use gap so the
transport connects only to an approved

21
00:01:07.861 --> 00:01:11.369
address while preserving hostname-based
TLS and HTTP identity.

22
00:01:11.449 --> 00:01:15.404
Then constrain the request itself: method,
headers, credentials, time,

23
00:01:15.484 --> 00:01:20.140
redirect count, request body, response
bytes, decompression, and expected content.

24
00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:23.728
Resource controls limit abuse but do not
validate a destination.

25
00:01:23.808 --> 00:01:25.658
Finally, make the policy durable.

26
00:01:25.738 --> 00:01:29.118
Route outbound operations through
reviewable abstractions,

27
00:01:29.198 --> 00:01:32.834
test changing destination states, log
sanitized policy outcomes,

28
00:01:32.914 --> 00:01:36.996
and constrain workload egress through
firewalls, proxies, segmentation,

29
00:01:37.076 --> 00:01:38.352
or service identities.

30
00:01:38.432 --> 00:01:42.769
Network controls contain mistakes but
cannot enforce business authorization;

31
00:01:42.849 --> 00:01:47.186
application checks understand the
operation but should not be the only barrier.

32
00:01:47.266 --> 00:01:51.157
A secure outbound request is not defined
by the URL the client submitted.

33
00:01:51.237 --> 00:01:55.638
It is defined by the destination the
server has explicitly permitted and actually

34
00:01:55.718 --> 00:01:56.420
connects to.
